      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. This process is crucial for growth, development, and reproduction in living organisms. To understand how mitosis works, let's break it down into its individual stages. During interphase, the cell grows, replicates its DNA, and prepares for cell division. As the cell prepares, the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, and the nuclear envelope breaks down in prophase.\n\nAs the chromosomes line up at the center of the cell in metaphase, the cell is poised for separation. In anaphase, the sister chromatids separate, moving to opposite poles of the cell. Finally, in telophase, the nuclear envelope reforms, and the chromosomes uncoil to form chromatin. The result is two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell, each with the same set of chromosomes.\n\nThis process is essential for the growth and development of multicellular organisms, as it allows cells to replace damaged or dying cells and maintain tissue homeostasis. By understanding how mitosis works, you can appreciate the intricate mechanisms that govern the behavior of living cells.\n\nI've tried to integrate the concepts of \"helpful,\" \"under",
